        "\n",
        "# 11 `geopandas`\n",
        "\n",
        "GeoPandas is a Python library that extends the `pandas` library by adding support for geospatial data. In this lesson we will introduce the `geopandas` library to work with vector data. We will also make our first map. \n",
        "\n",
        "<!-- TODO\n",
        "## Learning objectives\n",
        "-->\n",
        "\n",
        "## About the data\n",
        "\n",
        "Wild pigs (*Sus scrofa*) are a destructive invasive species in California, causing significant environmental and agricultural damage. Introduced to the region in the 1700s, they have since spread across the state, impacting native ecosystems through habitat destruction, soil disturbance due to their rooting behavior, and competition with native wildlife for food @finzel_wild_2015. This widespread damage has made managing wild pig populations a critical issue for conservation and agricultural communities in California.\n",
        "\n",
        "![Wild pig in Santa Teresa Park, CA. [Photo by Don DeBold](https://www.flickr.com/photos/ddebold/52936296848)](/book/images/lesson-10/wild_pig.png){width=\"70%\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "In this lesson we will use simplified point data about wild pig occurrence records in California from the [Global Biodiversity Information Facility (GBIF)](https://www.gbif.org). \n",
        "GBIF is an international network and data platform that provides open access to biodiversity data from around the world. By aggregating data from multiple sources, including museums, research institutions, and citizen science initiatives like iNaturalist, GBIF enables scientists, policymakers, and the public to explore and analyze species distribution and environmental trends. \n",
        "\n",
        "The data we will use today has been simplified from the complete *Sus scrofa* occurrence data accessed through the GBIF website @gbiforg_user_occurrence_2023. \n",
        "This data is shared under a [Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial 4.0 (CC BY-NC 4.0)](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/) license.\n",
        "\n",
        "Each row in the data is one occurrence record. \n",
        "The column names were shortened to fit the 10-character limit of shapefile column names:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Column | Description |\n",
        "|--------|-------------|\n",
        "| `gbifID` | Unique identifier of the record in GBIF. The full record is at `https://www.gbif.org/occurrence/` followed by this ID. |\n",
        "| `species` | Scientific name of the species |\n",
        "| `state` | State where the record was made |\n",
        "| `individual` | Number of individuals recorded |\n",
        "| `day`, `month`, `year` | Date of the record |\n",
        "| `inst` | Code of the institution that provided the record, for example a museum or a citizen science platform |\n",
        "| `collection` | Code of the collection within that institution |\n",
        "| `catalogNum` | Identifier of the record in the institution's catalog |\n",
        "| `identified` | Person or institution who identified the species |\n",
        "| `geometry` | Location of the record |\n",
        "\n",
        "We will also use a shapefile with the boundary of the state of California, which has a single feature: the outline of the state. \n",
        "This boundary comes from the US Census Bureau's 2016 TIGER/Line shapefiles @us_census_bureau_tiger_2016 and was converted to a different CRS for this lesson. \n",
        "We will use it as a base layer for our maps, to see where the wild pig records are located within California.\n",
        "\n",
        "Both datasets are in the `gbif_sus_scrofa_california` and `ca_state_boundary` folders of the course's [shared data folder]({{< var shared-data-folder >}}).\n",
        "\n",
        "## `geopandas`\n",
        "In this lesson we will introduce the `geopandas` @jordahl_geopandasgeopandas_2020 Python package. `geopandas` extends `pandas` to work with geospatial data. This is the main package we will be working with in the next few lessons.\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's start by importing the necessary packages and our data:"
      ],
      "id": "a26f2747"
    },
