        "\n",
        "## Visualizing our data\n",
        "\n",
        "### `plot()`\n",
        "\n",
        "Similarly to a `pandas.DataFrame`, a `geopandas.GeoDataFrame` has a `plot()` method that we can call directly to create a quick, static view of our data.\n",
        "The geospatial information of the `geopandas.GeoDataFrame` will be used to create the axes of the plot.\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's take a quick look at our recent pigs data:"
      ],
      "id": "ff11a704"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "pigs_recent.plot()"
      ],
      "id": "b888d7c7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "### Using `matplotlib`\n",
        "\n",
        "Going forward, we will make more complex visualizations where we add different layers to a map and customize it. \n",
        "To do this, we will use `matplotlib` via its `pyplot` interface for creating visualizations. You can review the `matplotlib` basics in this [previous lesson](/book/chapters/lesson-18-linear-regression/lesson-18-linear-regression.qmd).\n",
        "\n",
        "#### Example\n",
        "\n",
        "When using matplotlib, it can be useful to think of creating a plot as adding layers to an axes. In this case, the first layer that we want to add to our axes is the `pigs_recent` point data. \n",
        "We can plot our data using `matplotlib` like this:"
      ],
      "id": "7da1f2ea"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Initialize empty figure and axes\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add pigs point plot to our figure's axes\n",
        "pigs_recent.plot(ax=ax)\n",
        "\n",
        "# Display figure\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "0f631c27",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Then some simple customization for our pigs data could look like this:"
      ],
      "id": "8f765ec0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Initialize empty figure\n",
        "fig, ax = plt.subplots()\n",
        "\n",
        "# Add data to axes\n",
        "pigs_recent.plot(ax=ax,         # Add plot to axes\n",
        "                 alpha=0.5,     # Adjust transparency\n",
        "                 color='brown'  # Update point color\n",
        "                 )\n",
        "\n",
        "# Update axes \n",
        "ax.set_title('Reported \"Sus scrofa\" sightings in CA (2020-2023)')\n",
        "ax.set_xlabel('Longitude')\n",
        "ax.set_ylabel('Latitude')\n",
        "\n",
        "# Display figure\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "7ab64345",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
